What steps can reproduce the bug?
On Node, the socket an https request handler gets is a tls.TLSSocket, so req.socket.getPeerCertificate() returns the client's certificate. On Bun it's a plain Socket and the method isn't there. Bun's own tls.createServer is fine, so this is specific to node:https.
Run from the repo root with both runtimes:
import { readFileSync } from "fs";
import https from "node:https";
import tls from "node:tls";
import { join } from "path";

const FX = "test/js/node/tls/fixtures";
const rd = f => readFileSync(join(FX, f), "utf8");
const OPTS = {
  key: rd("agent10-key.pem"),
  cert: rd("agent10-cert.pem"),
  ca: rd("ca5-cert.pem"),
  requestCert: true,
  rejectUnauthorized: false,
};

const server = https.createServer(OPTS, (req, res) => {
  const s = req.socket;
  res.end(
    JSON.stringify({
      ctor: s.constructor?.name,
      isTLSSocket: s instanceof tls.TLSSocket,
      getPeerCertificate: typeof s.getPeerCertificate,
      authorized: s.authorized,
      peerCN: s.getPeerCertificate?.()?.subject?.CN ?? null,
    }),
  );
});
await new Promise(r => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", r));

const body = await new Promise(res => {
  const s = tls.connect({
    host: "127.0.0.1",
    port: server.address().port,
    rejectUnauthorized: false,
    key: rd("ec10-key.pem"),
    cert: rd("ec10-cert.pem"),
  });
  let got = "";
  s.on("secureConnect", () => s.write("GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n"));
  s.on("data", c => (got += c));
  s.on("close", () => res((got.split("\r\n\r\n")[1] || "").trim()));
});
console.log(body);
server.close();
What is the expected behavior?
Node v26.7.0:
{"ctor":"TLSSocket","isTLSSocket":true,"getPeerCertificate":"function","authorized":true,"peerCN":"agent10.example.com"}

What do you see instead?
{"ctor":"Socket","isTLSSocket":false,"getPeerCertificate":"undefined","authorized":true,"peerCN":null}

authorized is correct, so the boolean half of the client-certificate state is wired up. Only the certificate itself is unreachable.
Additional information
tls.createServer on Bun gives a real TLSSocket and getPeerCertificate() returns the certificate, so TLSSocket.prototype.getPeerCertificate (src/js/node/tls.ts) and the underlying accessor both work. It's the socket that node:https hands the request handler that isn't a TLSSocket.
