What steps can reproduce the bug?
When you call net.Socket#unref() before connect(), Node defers the unref to the connect event. The pending connection keeps the event loop alive. Bun applies the unref immediately, so the process exits before the connection completes.
Save this file as minimal.mjs and run bun minimal.mjs.
import net from "node:net";

const server = net.createServer(() => {});
server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", () => {
  const port = server.address().port;

  const s = new net.Socket();
  s.unref(); // before connect()
  s.connect(port, "127.0.0.1", () => {
    process.stderr.write("CONNECTED\n");
    s.destroy();
    server.close();
  });
});
server.unref();

process.on("exit", (c) => process.stderr.write(`exit ${c}\n`));
Run node minimal.mjs to compare.
This breaks testcontainers. The Ryuk reaper calls socket.unref() before .connect() in build/reaper/reaper.js. Every testcontainers program exits with code 0 during container startup and runs no tests.
What is the expected behavior?
The process stays alive until the connection completes.
CONNECTED
exit 0
Node v22.22.2 and Bun 1.3.14 both give this output.
What do you see instead?
The connect callback never runs.
There is no error, no exception, and no unhandled rejection. The exit code is 0, so the failure is silent.
Additional information
This is a regression. Bun 1.3.14 is correct. Bun 1.4.0-canary.1 is not.
The canary adds one line to net.Socket.prototype.unref. Output of Socket.prototype.unref.toString():
// Bun 1.3.14 (correct)
function unref() {
  let socket = this._handle;
  if (!socket)
    return this.once("connect", this.unref), this;
  return socket.unref(), this;
}

// Bun 1.4.0-canary.1 (broken)
function unref() {
  this[kUserUnrefed] = !0;   // <-- added
  let socket = this._handle;
  if (!socket)
    return this.once("connect", this.unref), this;
  return socket.unref(), this;
}
The JavaScript deferral through once("connect") is still present in both versions. The canary sets kUserUnrefed before the handle exists, and the connect path reads this flag immediately.
Other notes:

beforeExit fires with code 0. The event loop drains normally.
The reverse order works. If you call connect() first and unref() inside the connect callback, Bun is correct.
A socket that is never unreffed works.

