What is the problem this feature would solve?
Frameworks that embed Bun.serve as their dev server need to run bundler plugins on the dev-bundling path (HTML imports / routes), and today those plugins can only be configured through the user's bunfig.toml ([serve.static] plugins). There is no programmatic way to pass them, and a runtime Bun.plugin() never reaches the dev-server bundler.
Our concrete case (nifra, a Bun-native full-stack framework): the production client build uses a Bun.build() plugin to replace *.fn.ts server-function modules with RPC stubs, so their bodies (DB handles, secrets, imports) never reach the browser. In dev we embed Bun.serve for its native HMR + Fast Refresh, but we cannot apply that same plugin, so serving any app that uses server functions would ship the raw module - a security issue. We currently refuse to start rather than leak, which blocks Bun's own dev server from being our default dev loop.
Owning/patching the user's bunfig.toml from a framework CLI is not workable: it is the user's file, it is global to their project, and it applies to every bun invocation, not just our dev server process.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Accept dev-bundling plugins programmatically, for example:
Bun.serve({
  routes: { "/": html },
  development: {
    hmr: true,
    plugins: [myStubPlugin], // same BunPlugin shape Bun.build accepts
  },
})
Any equivalent shape would work for us (top-level plugins, per-HTMLBundle option, or letting plugin()-registered runtime plugins reach the dev bundler).
Verified today on Bun 1.3.14:

bunfig.toml [serve.static] plugins = ["./stub-plugin.ts"] DOES apply to Bun.serve HTML-import bundling (our onLoad stub replaced the module in the served chunk) - so the machinery exists; only the programmatic entry point is missing.
A runtime Bun.plugin({ setup(build) { build.onLoad(...) } }) registered before Bun.serve does NOT fire for dev-server-bundled modules.

