bun audit: no way to scope by dependency class (no --production / --omit=dev), and no nested overrides to remediate a deep transitive dep
Summary
We added a dependency-audit gate to our build system (it runs bun audit --json, classifies findings, and fails the build on an ungated high+ advisory). Building it surfaced two gaps in bun audit / overrides that, together, make the "audit → remediate" loop harder than it needs to be:

bun audit cannot be scoped to a dependency class. There is no --production / --omit=dev equivalent, so the output mixes advisories in shipped runtime deps with advisories in dev-only tooling. Those have very different blast radii and a tool cannot tell them apart from the JSON.
overrides cannot express a nested/path-scoped constraint (npm's "a > b": "1.2.3" form), so remediating one transitive path forces a global pin.

Both are the same underlying need: act on a specific dependency path, not on a package name globally.
1. bun audit --production (or --omit=dev)
bun audit --help currently offers --audit-level and --ignore, but nothing to filter by dependency class.
Why it matters. For a published library, an advisory in a runtime dependencies entry reaches every consumer; an advisory in a devDependency (a linter, a test runner) never leaves the maintainer's machine. Any policy that wants to treat those differently — block one, report the other — has to reconstruct reachability itself by walking the tree from each dependencies root, which is a lot of machinery to answer a question the resolver already knows.
npm audit --omit=dev and yarn npm audit --environment production both expose this.
Ideally the JSON would also carry the dependency class (and, even better, the path) per advisory, so a tool can make the distinction without a second pass:
{
  "some-pkg": [
    {
      "id": 1234567,
      "severity": "high",
      "dev": true,                                  // ← or "class": "dev"
      "paths": ["eslint > some-pkg"]                // ← how it got into the tree
    }
  ]
}
2. Nested / path-scoped overrides
Today overrides are keyed by package name and apply globally:
{ "overrides": { "brace-expansion": ">=5.0.8" } }
npm supports scoping an override to a dependency path:
{
  "overrides": {
    "foo": {
      "bar": "1.2.3"      // only bar *under* foo
    },
    "baz > qux": "2.0.0"  // path form
  }
}
Why it matters for remediation. When an advisory affects one package that appears in the tree via several parents, the minimal fix

