On Linux, fs.watch never reports the destination of an atomic save — write a temp file, then rename() it over the target. Only the temp file's name is delivered, and it no longer exists by the time the callback runs, so the change is effectively invisible.
This is what vim, VS Code, and most editors and coding agents do on every save, so on Linux it is the normal way a file changes.
import { mkdtempSync, watch, writeFileSync, renameSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";

const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "atomic-save-"));
const target = join(dir, "app.tsx");
writeFileSync(target, "v0");

const events = [];
const w = watch(dir, (event, filename) => events.push(`${event}:${filename}`));

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
await sleep(300);

// An atomic save: write a temp file, rename it over the target.
const tmp = join(dir, "app.tsx.tmp");
writeFileSync(tmp, "v1");
renameSync(tmp, target);

await sleep(500);
w.close();

console.log("events:", events.join(" | ") || "(none)");
console.log("saw destination 'app.tsx':", events.some((e) => e.endsWith(":app.tsx")));
rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
What is the expected behavior?
An event naming app.tsx — the file that actually changed. The kernel provides it. Reading the same directory with raw inotify shows the destination name is right there:
CREATE      app.tsx.tmp
MODIFY      app.tsx.tmp
CLOSE_WRITE app.tsx.tmp
MOVED_FROM  app.tsx.tmp
MOVED_TO    app.tsx        ← never surfaced by fs.watch

Node reports rename:app.tsx.tmp | rename:app.tsx.
What do you see instead?
events: rename:app.tsx.tmp
saw destination 'app.tsx': false

Only the temp name. Identical with { recursive: true }.
Measured across 70 atomic saves: the destination name arrived 0 times, with {recursive: true} and without, and with delays of 0 / 50 / 150 / 400ms inserted between the write and the rename. It does surface in the rare case where the reader happens to split the MOVED_FROM/MOVED_TO pair across two read() calls, which makes the failure look intermittent in real applications even though it is close to deterministic.
A brand-new file created by atomic save is affected the same way — a Write-then-rename produces two events, both naming the temp file, and none naming the created file.
