What steps can reproduce the bug?
When a Bun.serve fetch handler returns a final response before consuming the entire request body, Bun neither drains the remaining body nor closes the connection nor sends Connection: close. The unread body bytes stay in the socket buffer, so the next request on that keep-alive connection is misframed and rejected as 400 Bad Request.
Run this script (raw TCP keep-alive client, no HTTP library involved):
const server = Bun.serve({
  port: 0,
  async fetch(request) {
    const { pathname } = new URL(request.url);

    if (pathname === "/early") {
      // Read one chunk, then respond — the rest of the body is left unread.
      if (request.body) await request.body.getReader().read();
      return new Response("early response", { status: 413 });
    }

    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, pathname }), {
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  },
});

const chunk = (s: string) => `${s.length.toString(16)}\r\n${s}\r\n`;

let buffer = "";
const socket = await Bun.connect({
  hostname: "127.0.0.1",
  port: server.port,
  socket: { data: (_s, d) => { buffer += new TextDecoder().decode(d); } },
});

// Request 1: chunked POST the handler answers early, without reading it all.
socket.write(
  "POST /early HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n\r\n" +
  chunk("first-chunk-") + chunk("unread-remainder-of-the-body-")
);
await Bun.sleep(150);
const first = buffer; buffer = "";

// Request 2: a normal GET, REUSING the same keep-alive connection.
socket.write("GET /health HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\n\r\n");
await Bun.sleep(150);
const second = buffer;

console.log("--- Response 1 ---\n" + first.trim());
console.log("\n--- Response 2 (same connection) ---\n" + (second.trim() || "(no response / socket closed)"));

socket.end();
server.stop(true);
process.exit(0);
What is the expected behavior?
Per RFC 7230 §6.3.1, a server that sends a response before reading the full request body must either read (drain) the remainder before reusing the connection, or close it (e.g. via Connection: close). So the second request should either succeed with 200 {"ok":true,"pathname":"/health"} on a drained connection, or the first response should carry Connection: close and the connection be closed. Responding early on an unread body should never be able to corrupt a subsequent request.
