When a node:http2 client connects to a server that accepts the TCP connection but never completes the HTTP/2 handshake (never sends its SETTINGS frame), a request made on that session is never failed — not when the request is made, and not even after the server subsequently resets the connection. The session emits close, but the pending ClientHttp2Stream never emits error or close, so anything awaiting that request hangs forever.
Node.js fails the pending request promptly in the identical scenario.
This half-open shape is common in the real world: a backend process that is crashing/restarting (the listener socket accepts, the process never serves), or a load balancer routing to a dead endpoint. We hit this in production via a gRPC client built on node:http2 — requests issued during a backend restart were orphaned forever with no error.
