What steps can reproduce the bug?
When passing a negative fractional timestamp as a string (e.g., "-1.5" seconds), Bun's internal from_seconds math incorrectly splits the values into a timespec.
tv_sec: seconds as _,                           // -1.5 becomes -1
tv_nsec: (seconds.rem_euclid(1.0) * NS_PER_S) as _, // becomes 500_000_000
Because -1 second plus +0.5 seconds equals -0.5 seconds, Bun offsets the file modification time by a full second closer to the epoch.
Reproducer
import fs from 'node:fs';

const file = 'bug1_test.txt';
fs.writeFileSync(file, 'test');

// Set time to -1.5 seconds before 1970
fs.utimesSync(file, "-1.5", "-1.5");

const stats = fs.statSync(file);
console.log("Expected mtime (ms): -1500");
console.log("Actual mtime (ms):  ", stats.mtime.getTime());

fs.unlinkSync(file);
What is the expected behavior?
The file timestamp should be set exactly to -1500 ms (1969-12-31T23:59:58.500Z).
What do you see instead?
Bun sets the timestamp to -500 ms (1969-12-31T23:59:59.500Z), wrong by one second due to the incorrect truncation math.
Additional information
Sidenote 1
Note that, for numbers instead of strings, i.e. fs.utimesSync(file, -1.5, -1.5);, to stay bug-compatible to NodeJS (because imho that's an 11-year old bug in NodeJS), the behavior should still be to set the current time.
Sidenote 2
The if-clause here is dead code, because .rem_euclid is guaranteed to return a positive result:
#[cfg(not(windows))]
fn from_milliseconds(milliseconds: f64) -> TimeLike {
    let mut sec: f64 = milliseconds.div_euclid(MS_PER_S);
    let mut nsec: f64 = milliseconds.rem_euclid(MS_PER_S) * NS_PER_MS;

    if nsec < 0.0 {
        nsec += NS_PER_S;
        sec -= 1.0;
    }

    libc::timespec {
        tv_sec: sec as _,
        tv_nsec: nsec as _,
    }
}

