When controller.abort(reason) is called from inside a ReadableStream's cancel() callback (invoked natively via reader.cancel()), and the signal has no "abort" listener attached, signal.reason intermittently becomes undefined when read on a later event-loop turn — even though both the AbortController and its signal remain strongly referenced the whole time, and signal.aborted is still true.
Run the script below with bun repro.ts:
async function once(attachListener: boolean): Promise<boolean> {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  if (attachListener) {
    controller.signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {});
  }

  const stream = new ReadableStream({
    cancel() {
      controller.abort(new DOMException("The client disconnected.", "AbortError"));
    },
  });

  const reader = stream.getReader();
  await Bun.sleep(10);
  await reader.cancel(new Error("client walked away"));
  await Bun.sleep(10);

  // true = reason lost. signal.aborted is still true at this point.
  return controller.signal.aborted && controller.signal.reason === undefined;
}

for (const attachListener of [false, true]) {
  let lost = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 60; i += 1) {
    if (await once(attachListener)) lost += 1;
  }
  console.log(`${attachListener ? "with no-op abort listener" : "no listener"}: reason lost in ${lost}/60 iterations`);
}
Observed across five consecutive runs on 1.3.14:
no listener              : reason lost in 3–6/60 iterations (varies per run; GC timing)
with no-op abort listener: reason lost in 0/60 iterations   (every run)

The stream-cancel origin is load-bearing: the same abort issued from plain async code (no stream involved) never loses the reason — 0/60 even under Bun.gc(true).
What is the expected behavior?
Per the WHATWG DOM spec, once a signal is aborted, signal.reason permanently returns the exact value passed to abort() for the signal's lifetime. Whether an "abort" listener happens to be attached should have no effect on the retention of the reason.
What do you see instead?
Intermittently (roughly 5–10% of iterations), signal.aborted === true but signal.reason === undefined — the reason object has been garbage-collected out from under the live signal. Attaching any no-op "abort" listener before the abort eliminates the loss completely (0/60 in every run), which suggests the reason is held in a native slot t

