What is the problem this feature would solve?
Currently, reading and writing files in server-side JavaScript always involves significant overhead. If a developer wants to process a massive file (e.g., gigabytes of logs, a local vector database, or large machine learning models), the data must travel from the Disk -> OS Page Cache -> Rust/JSC Internal Buffer -> JavaScript ArrayBuffer.
This traditional streaming or buffering approach has two fatal flaws for extreme-performance applications:

Memory Bloat & GC Thrashing: Loading chunks of data constantly allocates and deallocates memory on the JS heap, putting immense pressure on the Garbage Collector.
Copying Overhead: Data is copied multiple times across the user-space and kernel-space boundaries.

If developers want to build high-performance database engines or memory-intensive applications in pure TypeScript, they need a way to access files as if they were just contiguous blocks of RAM, completely eliminating serialization and copying.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
I propose exposing the POSIX mmap (Memory-Mapped Files) system call directly to JavaScript via the previously proposed Bun.unsafe namespace.
This API would map a file descriptor directly into virtual memory and return a Bun.unsafe.Pointer. Developers can then read and write directly to the physical disk by simply manipulating the pointer offset in O(1) time. When combined with Bun.Struct, developers could instantly map complex C-ABI compatible data structures directly onto disk without a single serialization step.
