What is the problem this feature would solve?
With Bun's underlying architecture fully migrating to Rust, a solid foundation for memory safety and extreme performance has been established. However, when building high-performance systems in JavaScript/TypeScript (such as custom database drivers, high-throughput network parsers, or FFI bindings), developers still hit a hard ceiling: Garbage Collection (GC) thrashing and memory copying overhead.
Currently, while ArrayBuffer and DataView are available, they still require allocating JavaScript objects. When processing millions of WebSocket frames or parsing dense TCP byte streams from a database, the overhead of crossing the Native-to-JS boundary, allocating typed arrays, and triggering JavaScriptCore (JSC) GC pauses becomes the primary performance bottleneck.
To compete head-to-head with raw Rust or Go on the server side, server-side JavaScript desperately needs a mechanism to explicitly manage memory and manipulate raw pointers, thereby completely bypassing the memory allocation interference of the JS runtime.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
I propose introducing a brand new Bun.unsafe API namespace. This aligns perfectly with Rust's core philosophy: guarantee memory safety by default, but provide an explicit unsafe escape hatch for system-level performance tuning and raw memory manipulation.
This API would expose raw pointers and off-heap memory allocation capabilities directly to JavaScript.
