Summary
We believe Worker.terminate() does not synchronize with in-flight fetch() tasks before tearing down the worker's VM/event loop. When a fetch() running on Bun's dedicated HTTP I/O thread resolves (successfully or with an error) after its originating Worker has already been terminated, the result gets pushed onto that worker's now-dead event loop queue. We've captured two different crash signatures that both fit this shape, and a repro that's fast and reliable on Linux — not yet reproduced on Windows after significantly more punishment.
Evidence for the root cause
FetchTasklet::enqueue_concurrent documents its own load-bearing assumption:
// src/runtime/webcore/fetch/FetchTasklet.rs:299-310
/// Centralises the `(*vm.event_loop()).enqueue_task_concurrent(..)` raw
/// deref. `event_loop()` returns a self-ptr into the VirtualMachine that
/// is valid for the VM's lifetime; `enqueue_task_concurrent` takes `&self`
/// and is thread-safe (lock-free MPSC push). ...
#[inline]
fn enqueue_concurrent(vm: &VirtualMachine, task: core::ptr::NonNull<ConcurrentTask>) {
    vm.event_loop_shared().enqueue_task_concurrent(task);
}
EventLoop::enqueue_task_concurrent has a debug-only check for exactly the condition that breaks that assumption:
// src/jsc/event_loop.rs:960-971
pub fn enqueue_task_concurrent(&self, task: core::ptr::NonNull<ConcurrentTaskItem>) {
    if cfg!(debug_assertions) {
        if self.vm_ref().has_terminated {
            panic!("EventLoop.enqueueTaskConcurrent: VM has terminated");
        }
    }
    self.concurrent_tasks.push(task);
    self.wakeup();
}
So the codebase already knows "the VM may have terminated by the time this runs" is a real, anticipated condition — worth a panic! in debug builds — but that check compiles out in release/canary builds. Instead of panicking, it falls through to self.concurrent_tasks.push(task), a lock-free UnboundedQueue, potentially belonging to a VM that's already torn down.
