What happens
On macOS arm64, bun:ffi lays out stack-passed arguments using standard-AAPCS64 8-byte slots — one slot per argument. But Apple's arm64 ABI deviates from standard AAPCS64 here: stack arguments are packed at their natural size and alignment, so two consecutive C ints share a single 8-byte slot.
Any signature whose 9th-or-later integer-class arguments put a sub-8-byte type before further arguments hands the callee shifted garbage. For:
int f(void* res, uint64_t a, uint64_t b,
      int s0, int s1, int p0, int p1, int d0,   // x0..x7 (registers)
      int d1, int g, uint64_t stream);          // stack
the callee (clang, Apple ABI) expects d1 @ sp+0 (4 B), g @ sp+4 (4 B), stream @ sp+8. Bun writes d1 @ sp+0, g @ sp+8, stream @ sp+16 — so the callee reads g = 0 (the empty upper half of d1's slot) and stream = <g's value>.
We hit this binding mlx_conv2d from mlx-c (exactly this signature shape): the shifted read turns the mlx_stream handle into a small integer and mlx segfaults inside the call at ~0x1. It looks exactly like a bad binding, not an ABI bug, which makes it nasty to diagnose. Crash report from the real call: https://bun.report/1.3.14/Ma10d9b296AggggC____my5ilDmy5ilDurh5kDuov5tCmkk51Cuw8+I+6431Cmkl51CmpyoyC+0xoyCu6jlzCm6voyC+8l11C+/y34C+qy34C+5sy6CA2AC
