What happened?
RFC 6455 5.4 forbids starting a new data message while a fragmented message is still in progress: after a data frame with FIN=0, only continuation frames (opcode 0) and control frames may arrive until the message completes, and a server must fail the connection otherwise. Bun.serve's guard for this has two holes, so a second, unrelated data frame gets appended to the in-progress fragment buffer and the message() handler receives the concatenation of two messages the peer never sent as one.
Both reproduce with raw frames against Bun.serve (no compression involved). Shared harness:
import net from "node:net";

const received: string[] = [];
const server = Bun.serve({
  port: 0,
  fetch(req, server) {
    if (server.upgrade(req)) return;
    return new Response("no", { status: 400 });
  },
  websocket: {
    message(ws, m) {
      received.push(Buffer.from(m as Buffer).toString());
    },
  },
});

function frame(opcode: number, payload: Buffer, fin: boolean): Buffer {
  const mask = Buffer.from([1, 2, 3, 4]);
  const masked = Buffer.from(payload.map((b, i) => b ^ mask[i % 4]));
  return Buffer.concat([Buffer.from([(fin ? 0x80 : 0) | opcode, 0x80 | payload.length]), mask, masked]);
}

const sock = net.connect(server.port, "127.0.0.1");
await new Promise<void>(resolve => {
  let buf = Buffer.alloc(0);
  sock.on("data", c => {
    buf = Buffer.concat([buf, c]);
    if (buf.includes("\r\n\r\n")) resolve();
  });
  sock.write(
    "GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\nConnection: Upgrade\r\nUpgrade: websocket\r\n" +
      "Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13\r\nSec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\r\n\r\n",
  );
});

A new BINARY frame inside a fragmented BINARY message is accepted (the guard only catches TEXT):

sock.write(Buffer.concat([frame(0x2, Buffer.from("he"), false), frame(0x2, Buffer.from("llo"), true)]));
// message() receives "hello"; expected: no message, connection failed (ws closes 1002)

Any new data frame is accepted if a control frame was interleaved first, because the control frame's FIN overwrites the "previous data frame was not final" state the guard relies on. The interleaved ping itself is legal; the TEXT frame after it is not:

sock.write(Buffer.concat([
  frame(0x2, Buffer.from("abc"), false),
  frame(0x9, Buffer.alloc(0), true),
  frame(0x1, Buffer.from("xyz"), true),
]));
