What is the problem this feature would solve?
function is a rather long keyword. So having many things that use function adds a lot of bytes to the final file when we have arrow functions now. The only difference is that arrow functions "close over" the external this, whereas a function function can have a new this reassigned.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Bun's minifier should walk the AST of all function functions (recursing into arrow functions, but not recursing into other function functions or classes) and if it doesn't use this directly, it should rewrite the function into an arrow function and then hoisted to the top (since variables aren't hoisted automatically like function functions are):
// Can be rewritten:
function foo(a,b,c){something(a);somethingElse(b,c,function(){return this.f})}
// To:
var foo=(a,b,c)=>{something(a);somethingElse(b,c,function(){return this.f})}

// These cannot be rewritten as an arrow function because they use the outer function's this:
function foo(a,b,c){this.something(a);somethingElse(b,c)}
function foo(a,b,c){something(a);somethingElse(b,c,()=>this.f)}
The advantage of rewriting it is that since it's all toplevel, it can be chained with other vars and will only add a few characters per definition (= and ,) versus function  and var  since the chain was broken.
