TL;DR

A coordination object (pthread_mutex_t + pthread_cond_t, or any wait/notify primitive) is destroyed and freed while another thread is still inside the wait on it. POSIX: destroying a cond var with a blocked waiter is undefined behavior. The woken-but-slow waiter then touches freed memory → heap-use-after-free.
This is timing-sensitive: on fast native hardware the wake→return window is tiny so the owner's destroy "always" wins the race and you never see it. Under a slow / serialized scheduler the window widens and it crashes.
The PoC below is deterministic — it fires on the first run under ASan on both macOS arm64 and Linux aarch64 (musl/alpine). No proprietary library, no emulator.
In a real Bun workload (heavy JS allocation / GC churn concurrently with CPU-bound bun:ffi worker threads), Bun crashes with UAF signatures. Bun's own bun.report traces (below) symbolize the top frames into Bun/JSC code (subprocess.finalize, ParallelHelperClient::~ParallelHelperClient → JSC::Heap::~Heap, MarkedBlock GC), with the lower frames corrupted — the hallmark of a use-after-free.

Self-contained PoC (no external lib — please actually run this one)
