An empty update helper with a lowercase set reaches the engine as malformed SQL. The sqlite adapter's empty-update detection is a case-sensitive suffix check (query.endsWith("SET ")), so only the uppercase spelling throws the intended error:
await sql`update t set ${sql({ name: undefined })} where id = 1`;
// SQLiteError: near "where": syntax error
await sql`update t SET ${sql({ name: undefined })} where id = 1`;
// SyntaxError: Update needs to have at least one column
postgres and mysql gate this on a hasValues flag instead, so they throw the SyntaxError for both spellings; the asymmetry is sqlite-only.
Related: #32035 (the mysql ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE suffix check has the same case-sensitivity problem).

Split out of #32101 (review finding on the sibling code path), affects main.
transaction_sql.close({ timeout }) in src/js/bun/sql.ts has two problems in the branch where pending queries settle before the timer fires:

The drain branch only runs clearTimeout(timer); resolve();. Unlike the timer branch and the no-timeout path, it never issues ROLLBACK and never sets ReservedConnectionState.closed. When the sql.begin() callback then returns normally, onTransactionConnected proceeds to COMMIT (the closed gate in run_internal_transaction_sql is not set), so a transaction the user explicitly closed gets committed:

await sql.begin(async tx => {
  await tx`INSERT INTO t VALUES (1)`;
  const slow = tx`SELECT pg_sleep(0.01)`.execute();
  await tx.close({ timeout: 30 }); // queries drain first -> no ROLLBACK, closed not set
  await slow;
}); // COMMIT runs; the INSERT is persisted

The internal Promise.all([Promise.all(pending_queries), Promise.all(pending_savepoints)]).finally(...) chain is dropped, so if any pending query rejects during the grace period the rejection surfaces as an unhandledRejection (process exits nonzero) even when the user handles their own query promise.

#32101 fixed the identical shape in reserved_sql.close({ timeout }) (converge all branches on one idempotent close helper, wait with Promise.allSettled, single settled handler). The transaction variant needs the same treatment, plus care around BEFORE_COMMIT_OR_ROLLBACK_COMMAND and rollback failures, and its own tests, so it was left out of that PR.

