What does the bug do?
When a class contains any decorated member, standard decorator lowering rewrites private member accesses to __privateGet/__privateMethod WeakMap helpers. Optional chains that pass through such a lowered private access lose their short-circuiting: the nullish check from ?. is not carried into the rewritten expression, so the helper is called with undefined and throws instead of the whole chain evaluating to undefined.
Repro
// repro.js (standard decorators, so no experimentalDecorators tsconfig)
function decorator() { return (v, ctx) => {}; }
class Foo {
  static #m = function () { return { Foo }; };
  @decorator() test(o) { return o?.Foo.#m(); }
}
try {
  console.log("result:", new Foo().test(undefined));
} catch (e) {
  console.log("threw:", e.constructor.name);
}
Expected (spec, and what node + esbuild's decorator lowering produce): result: undefined — o?.Foo.#m() is one optional chain, so a nullish o short-circuits the whole thing.
Actual with bun: threw: TypeError (Cannot read from private field), because the lowering emits
__privateGet(o?.Foo, _m).call(o?.Foo)
and o?.Foo evaluates to undefined without short-circuiting the enclosing call.
esbuild's lowering of the same input emits the nullish guard explicitly:
o == null ? void 0 : __privateGet(_a = o.Foo, _m).call(_a)
Notes
The rewrite lives in rewrite_private_accesses_in_expr in src/js_parser/lower/lower_decorators.rs; it currently has no handling for OptionalChain flags on the EIndex/ECall nodes it rewrites. Related but distinct from the receiver duplication fixed in #31426 (that PR does not change this behavior) and from the missed initializer rewrites in #28118.

