Summary
Three methods in BabyVec perform self.len += n as u32 with no overflow guard. While the overflow is unreachable through safe APIs due to assertions in grow_to(), the code lacks explicit local invariant enforcement. This is a code quality and consistency improvement.
Affected Code
src/bun_alloc/baby_vec.rs
// Line 333 — extend_from_slice
unsafe {
    ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(other.as_ptr(), self.ptr.as_ptr().add(self.len as usize), n);
    self.len += n as u32;  // no overflow check
}

// Line 232 — append (same pattern)
self.len += n as u32;

// Line 254 — prepend_from (same pattern)
self.len += src_len as u32;
Why the Overflow is Unreachable
All three methods call reserve() before incrementing len:
pub fn reserve(&mut self, additional: usize) {
    let need = self.len as usize + additional;  // 64-bit — no wrap
    if need > self.cap as usize {
        self.grow_to(need);  // calls grow_to if capacity exceeded
    }
}

fn grow_to(&mut self, at_least: usize) {
    assert!(at_least <= u32::MAX as usize, "BabyVec capacity overflow");
    // ↑ This assert fires FIRST, before any ZST check
    if Self::T_IS_ZST {
        return;  // ZST early-return happens AFTER the assert
    }
    // ... allocation logic
}
For non-ZST types: When len + additional > u32::MAX, reserve() calls grow_to() which panics at the assert. The self.len += n as u32 line never executes.
For ZST types: Same protection. Even though cap = u32::MAX and grow_to() has an early-return for ZSTs, the assert fires before the early-return check. When len approaches u32::MAX and you try to add more elements, grow_to() panics before the ZST branch is reached.
Why the Fix is Still Valuable
Even though the overflow is unreachable, adding checked_add improves the code:
1. Consistency with Existing Code
LinearFifo::ensure_unused_capacity (line 397) uses checked_add for the same pattern:
// src/collections/linear_fifo.rs:397
let total = self.count.checked_add(size).ok_or(AllocError)?;
self.ensure_total_capacity(total)
2. Code Clarity
Makes the invariant explicit at the mutation site. Future maintainers don't need to trace through reserve() → grow_to() to verify safety.
3. Defense-in-Depth
Protects against future refactoring that might:

Reorder the checks in grow_to()
Add a code path that bypasses reserve()
Change the ZST handling logic

4. Zero Runtime Cost
The optimizer can prove the panic is unreachable (since reserve() already guarantees the invariant), so the check compiles away in release bu

