Additional information
The root cause is in src/js/node/https.ts:
  var https = {
    // ...
    Server: http.Server,           // same reference, not a separate class
    createServer: http.createServer,
    // ...
  };
Related: #8704 was the same issue for https.Agent, fixed by #11826. This issue is the Server counterpart that was not addressed in that PR.
A possible fix would be to create a proper https.Server class similar to how #11826 handled https.Agent:
  function Server(options, requestListener) {
    if (!(this instanceof Server)) return new Server(options, requestListener);
    http.Server.$call(this, options, requestListener);
  }
  Server.prototype = Object.create(http.Server.prototype);

Summary
AtomicCell<T> in src/bun_core/atomic_cell.rs bounds its unsafe impl Send and unsafe impl Sync on T: Atom, but is missing the required T: Send constraint. As a result, a non-Send type T that implements Atom can be made cross-thread via AtomicCell<T>, violating Rust's aliasing and thread-safety guarantees.
The T: Atom bound narrowing landed in #30924 (EXP-098) is the correct direction but is insufficient on its own.
Proposed fix
// current
unsafe impl<T: Atom> Send for AtomicCell<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Atom> Sync for AtomicCell<T> {}

// correct
unsafe impl<T: Atom + Send> Send for AtomicCell<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Atom + Send> Sync for AtomicCell<T> {}
References

Identified during UB audit — see #30903
Partial mitigation (T: Atom bounds) landed in #30924 (EXP-098)
Raised by @coderabbitai code review on #30924
