Summary
Found in src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:1671 during the unsafe audit. A [u8; 128] stack buffer is pointer-cast to &mut [u8; 125], but wrote_len can reach 128.
let mut close_reason_buf = [0u8; 128];
// ... cursor writes up to 128 bytes into close_reason_buf ...
let wrote_len = cursor.position() as usize;  // can be up to 128

// SAFETY: close_reason_buf has 128 bytes; reinterpret first 125 as fixed array
let buf_ptr = close_reason_buf.as_mut_ptr().cast::<[u8; 125]>();
this.send_close_with_body(code, Some(unsafe { &mut *buf_ptr }), wrote_len);
send_close_with_body takes &mut [u8; 125] and body_len: usize. If wrote_len > 125, the function reads past the 125-byte reference's provenance → UB under Stacked Borrows.
Reachability
The RFC 6455 close frame reason phrase max is 123 bytes (+ 2 bytes status code = 125 total payload). But the code doesn't enforce this limit — cursor.position() can reach 128 if the caller passes a longer reason string through the C++ side.
Fix
Clamp wrote_len before passing:
let wrote_len = cursor.position() as usize;
let wrote_len = wrote_len.min(125);  // enforce RFC limit
Or error out if the close reason is too long (better for correctness):
if wrote_len > 125 {
    // Close reason too long — send truncated frame
    wrote_len = 125;
}
Or use debug_assert!(wrote_len <= 125) to catch this in debug builds.

