Found in src/sourcemap/InternalSourceMap.rs:209 during the unsafe audit. A Copy thin-pointer type has a free_owned() method that calls Box::from_raw with no runtime check — it relies entirely on programmer discipline.
#[derive(Copy, Clone)]
pub struct InternalSourceMap {
    pub data: *const u8,  // may point to heap OR borrowed memory
}

// TODO(port): conditional ownership — intentionally NOT impl Drop because
// InternalSourceMap is a Copy view and may borrow non-owned memory. Phase B
// should split into an owning newtype with impl Drop.
pub fn free_owned(self) {
    unsafe {
        drop(Box::<[u8]>::from_raw(core::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(
            self.data.cast_mut(), self.total_len(),
        )));
    }
}
Where it is called
Only from src/jsc/SavedSourceMap.rs (lines 278, 371), when evicting entries from the source map cache. The SAFETY comment at each call site says "blob was heap-allocated via put_mappings" — but this is an informal contract, not enforced.
What could go wrong
If InternalSourceMap is constructed from borrowed memory (e.g., the module graph section) and free_owned() is accidentally called:

Box::from_raw takes ownership of memory that was never heap-allocated
Drop of the Box calls the global allocator's free() on it
Result: use-after-free on the borrowed memory, or allocator corruption

This is a latent UAF bug gated only on programmer discipline.
Proposed fix (already noted in TODO)
Split into two types (the Rust standard pattern for views vs owners):
// Non-owning view (Copy, no Drop)
#[derive(Copy, Clone)]
pub struct InternalSourceMap<'a> {
    data: &'a [u8],
}

// Owning type (not Copy, impl Drop)
pub struct OwnedInternalSourceMap {
    blob: Box<[u8]>,
}

impl OwnedInternalSourceMap {
    fn as_view(&self) -> InternalSourceMap<'_> { ... }
    fn into_raw_parts(self) -> (*const u8, usize) { ... }
}

impl Drop for OwnedInternalSourceMap {
    fn drop(&mut self) { /* free heap memory */ }
}
Then SavedSourceMap::put_value stores OwnedInternalSourceMap::into_raw_parts(), and put_mappings wraps the Box directly. The table entry would need a discriminant to distinguish InternalSourceMap entries from ParsedSourceMap/SourceProviderMap entries — or the pointer tag scheme could encode ownership.
Alternatives if full refactor is too heavy

Minimal fix: make free_owned unsafe and document the precondition
Tagged pointer: use the low bit of data to distinguish heap (1) vs borrowed (0)
Phase B tracking: add an issue label / trac

Summary
Found in src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:1756 during the unsafe audit. A raw-pointer workaround bypasses the borrow checker because it cannot prove two references point to disjoint allocations.
// src/http_jsc/websocket_client.rs:1744-1756
let vm_ptr: *mut _ = vm;
// SAFETY: rare_data() returns &mut RareData from a SEPARATE Box allocation,
// so it does not overlap with &*vm_ptr (a shared &VirtualMachine).
unsafe { (*vm_ptr).rare_data().ws_client_group::<SSL>(&*vm_ptr) }
Why borrowck rejects this
rare_data(&mut self) borrows the entire VirtualMachine mutably from borrowck's perspective, and &*vm_ptr wants a shared borrow of the same struct — conflict.
Why it's actually sound (under Stacked Borrows / Tree Borrows)
rare_data returns self.rare_data.as_mut().unwrap() where rare_data: Option<Box<RareData>>. The returned &mut RareData points into the Box's separate heap allocation, not into VirtualMachine itself. Under Stacked Borrows' per-allocation model, &VirtualMachine and &mut RareData have different tags on different allocations, so no aliasing violation.
The fragility
The soundness depends on:

rare_data() always returning a reference to a separate allocation (currently a Box)
ws_client_group() never accessing vm.rare_data

These invariants are only documented in comments — the compiler doesn't enforce them. If rare_data() is refactored to use an inline field instead of a Box, this pattern silently becomes UB.
Suggestions


Document the invariant on rare_data():
/// SAFETY NOTE: callers rely on this returning a reference to a
/// SEPARATE heap allocation (Box), not to a field of VirtualMachine
/// itself. This enables raw-pointer borrow-splitting patterns.
pub fn rare_data(&mut self) -> &mut RareData { ... }


Consider #[field_projection] attributes (future Rust feature): annotate methods that only borrow specific fields, letting borrowck do field-level analysis.


Consider Miri CI check: run affected files under Miri to catch violations if the invariant ever breaks.


Audit similar patterns: this is the most prominent instance, but the triage found 27+ needs_dive entries — several likely share this pattern.


