What is the problem this feature would solve?
Bun.Transpiler.transformSync(code) returns the transformed code as a plain string. For TypeScript inputs, it strips type annotations, removes interface / type declarations, and collapses adjacent blank lines — so the output line numbers don't correspond to the input. Tools that build sourcemap chains around Bun.Transpiler (compile-to-intermediate workflows like AbsoluteJS's compileAngular.transpileFile, framework SFC pipelines that need TS-stripping per file, custom transformer plugins) can't recover the original line numbers and have to either:

Settle for stack traces that point at the transpiled intermediate JS (degraded DX — devs land on a generated file instead of the .ts they wrote).
Switch to a per-file Bun.build pass that's ~20–100× slower (real cost for projects with many small files).
Roll their own line-shift heuristic, which fails as soon as the transpiler does more than blank-line drops (e.g. whole-line interface removal, decorator-metadata reshape).

