What steps can reproduce the bug?
When using HTTP_PROXY with fetch(), Bun rewrites the absolute-form request URI to include :80 for http:// URLs. This causes failures with proxies that use the port in the request URI to determine the backend connection port.
curl (and Node's http.request) omit the default port from the request URI, per RFC 7230 §5.3.2, which says the absolute-form URI should be sent as-is (without injecting default ports).
Reproduction
1. Proxy spy (logs raw requests)
proxy-spy.mjs:
import net from "node:net";

const server = net.createServer((socket) => {
  socket.on("data", (data) => {
    console.log("=== RAW REQUEST ===");
    console.log(data.toString());
    console.log("=== END ===");
    socket.end("HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\nok\n");
  });
});

server.listen(9999, () => console.log("Proxy spy listening on :9999"));
2. Test script
repro.mjs:
const res = await fetch("http://example.com/test", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ hello: "world" }),
});
console.log(await res.text());
3. Run
Terminal 1:
node proxy-spy.mjs
Terminal 2:
# curl (correct behavior)
curl -x localhost:9999 http://example.com/test -d '{}'

# bun (incorrect behavior)
HTTP_PROXY=http://localhost:9999 bun repro.mjs
Results
curl sends:
POST http://example.com/test HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

Bun sends:
POST http://example.com:80/test HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

Note the :80 injected by Bun.
What is the expected behavior?
Bun should not inject the default port (:80 for http://, :443 for https://) into the absolute-form request URI when sending requests through an HTTP proxy. The URL should be forwarded as-is, matching curl and Node.js behavior.
What do you see instead?
No response
Additional information
Impact
Proxies that parse the port from the request URI to determine which port to connect to on the backend will attempt to connect on port 80 even when the actual service is on a different port (routed by hostname). This causes 503 / connection failures with proxies that would otherwise work fine with curl or Node.
The workaround is to add the port explicitly to the URL (e.g., http://host:443/path), but this shouldn't be necessary — the proxy should receive the URL as the client specified it.

