Description
When a JavaScript Date object is passed as a query parameter to Bun.SQL with prepare: false — either in a template-literal query or inside a row object passed to sql(rows) — Bun serializes it using Date.prototype.toString() (locale / timezone-dependent string) instead of Date.prototype.toISOString() (RFC 3339 / ISO 8601).
This does not reproduce with prepare: true (the default): when prepared statements are enabled, Bun receives the server's ParameterDescription before encoding the Bind message, so it has type information and uses the correct binary path. With prepare: false, Parse and Bind are pipelined with no type info available, and Date falls through to a toString() fallback.
Most databases, including PostgreSQL and GreptimeDB, cannot parse the locale format and return a parse error.
Reproduction
import { SQL } from 'bun';
const sql = new SQL('postgres://user@localhost:5432/db', { prepare: false });

const t = new Date('2024-01-15T12:30:45.000Z');

// In a WHERE clause parameter:
const rows = await sql`SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE created_at = ${t}`;
// Sends: "Mon Jan 15 2024 12:30:45 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)"
// PostgreSQL error: invalid input syntax for type timestamp

// In a row object passed to sql(rows):
await sql`INSERT INTO my_table ${sql([{ created_at: t }])}`;
// Same incorrect serialization
The workaround is to call .toISOString() manually at every parameter site, or to convert dates before building row objects:
// Workaround
await sql`SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE created_at = ${t.toISOString()}`;
Expected behavior
Date objects should be serialized as an ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 string when sent as query parameters — a format PostgreSQL-compatible databases can parse. Both other major JS drivers do this:

postgres.js hardcodes .toISOString() in src/types.js (types.date.serialize), always producing a UTC string ending in Z (e.g. "2024-01-15T12:30:45.000Z").
pg (node-postgres) builds an ISO 8601 string with the local timezone offset (e.g. "2024-01-15T12:30:45.000-05:00") — not identical to postgres.js, but still ISO 8601 and accepted by PostgreSQL.

Actual behavior
Bun serializes Date as the locale string produced by .toString(), e.g.:
Mon Jan 15 2024 12:30:45 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)

