What is the expected behavior?
All four cases should work with localhost. Bun's Happy Eyeballs implementation (PR #11206) correctly handles this for fetch() and Bun.connect(), but third-party libraries that use Node's net.createConnection() API don't benefit from it.
Node.js handles this at the runtime level via c-ares (RFC 6724) and autoSelectFamily (RFC 8305, enabled by default since Node 20), so libraries like postgres.js and ioredis work with localhost on Node without any changes.
What do you see instead?

net.Socket and fetch() work (Happy Eyeballs applied)
postgres.js hangs indefinitely (tries ::1, connection never completes)
ioredis fails with ECONNRESET or hangs

Additional information
DNS resolution confirms the root cause:
import dns from "dns";
dns.lookup("localhost", { all: true }, (err, addresses) => {
  console.log(addresses);
  // [ { address: '::1', family: 6 }, { address: '127.0.0.1', family: 4 } ]
});
::1 is returned first. Docker binds to 0.0.0.0 (IPv4 only), so the IPv6 attempt hangs.
Node.js solves this with autoSelectFamily: true (default since v20) in net.createConnection(), which implements Happy Eyeballs at the socket API level — meaning all libraries automatically benefit.
Bun's Happy Eyeballs (PR #11206) only covers Bun-native APIs (fetch, Bun.connect), not the Node.js net compatibility layer that third-party libraries use.
