Problem
When using CSS modules with @keyframes, the Bun bundler is renaming the @keyframes animation names to guarantee uniqueness (e.g. @keyframes slideInFromRight_DYpgJg). However, when referencing that animation in a class using the animation or animation-name property, the original, untransformed name is emitted (e.g. animation: slideInFromRight .3s ease-out;).

Reproduction
Given this CSS module (e.g. Animations.module.css):
@keyframes slideInFromRight {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateX(40px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateX(0);
  }
}

.forward {
  animation: slideInFromRight .3s ease-out;
}
Actual emitted CSS:
@keyframes slideInFromRight_DYpgJg {
  ...
}

.forward_DYpgJg {
  animation: slideInFromRight .3s ease-out;
}

How to reproduce:

Create a CSS module that defines an animation with @keyframes AnimationName { ... } and uses it in a selector using animation: AnimationName ...
Build using Bun's bundler
See that the outputted CSS renames the @keyframes but not the property reference, so animations do not play

What is the expected behavior?
The animation property should reference the correct, renamed unique keyframe (e.g. animation: slideInFromRight_DYpgJg .3s ease-out;), so that the class will trigger the right animation.
What do you see instead?
CSS emits a different name for the keyframes declaration and the property reference, breaking the animation (animation does not play).
Additional information
Analysis:

The code that prints the @keyframes name applies the CSS module suffix/renaming, but the code that prints the animation or animation-name property does not.
The two need to use the same transformation function so the reference matches the defined keyframes.
See KeyframesRule.toCss and animation.zig.

