Bun appears to misidentify the relative path as a directory, resulting in an error (e.g., EISDIR or a similar failure), rather than reading the file contents.
Additional information
Looking at the source code, there are inconsistencies in how file:// prefixes are stripped across the codebase. For example, in node_fs_watcher.zig and node_fs_stat_watcher.zig, the prefix "file://" (7 characters) is stripped with slice[6..], which retains an extra leading /:
// src/bun.js/node/node_fs_watcher.zig#L641
if (bun.strings.startsWith(slice, "file://")) {
    slice = slice[6..];  // should be slice[7..] — off-by-one
}
// src/bun.js/node/node_fs_stat_watcher.zig#L514
if (bun.strings.startsWith(slice, "file://")) {
    slice = slice[6..];  // same off-by-one
}
For fetch() with file:// URLs, the issue is likely related to how the URL is parsed. Per the URL specification:

file:///absolute/path — the triple slash indicates an absolute path on localhost
file://hostname/path — double slash followed by non-slash is interpreted as a hostname
file:./relative/path — a single colon should be used for relative paths (though this is non-standard)

When fetch("file://test.txt") is called, test.txt may be interpreted as a hostname rather than a file path, or the path resolution logic may produce an incorrect path that points to a directory instead of a file.
Workaround: Use absolute paths or Bun.file() instead:
import { resolve } from "path";
// Option 1: absolute file:// URL
const res = await fetch(new URL(`file://${resolve("test.txt")}`));

// Option 2: Use Bun.file() API (recommended)
const text = await Bun.file("test.txt").text();

