const blob = await archive.blob(); 
await Bun.write("backup.tar", blob);
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Bun.Archive should expose a way to produce a ReadableStream that generates the tarball on-the-fly as it is being consumed. This would allow streaming the archive directly to disk or a network request (e.g., S3 upload) without buffering the whole file.
const archive = new Bun.Archive("./large-folder");
const stream = archive.stream(); 

await Bun.write("backup.tar", stream);
What alternatives have you considered?
Bun.spawn(["tar", "-c", ...]): Piping the output of the system tar command. This is performant but platform-dependent (requires tar/gtar).
tar-fs / tar-stream: Using Node.js streams in user-land. This avoids OOM but is slower than a native implementation.

What is the problem this feature would solve?
