What is the problem this feature would solve?
Bun's built-in S3 client (Bun.S3Client) currently supports traditional S3 object storage operations like file read/write, presigned URLs, and listing. However, AWS recently announced general availability of S3 Vectors (December 2025), which introduces a completely new API for storing and querying vector embeddings natively in S3.
S3 Vectors uses a separate set of API operations (CreateVectorBucket, CreateVectorIndex, PutVectors, QueryVectors, etc.) that are not part of the standard S3 API. Currently, Bun users building AI applications with RAG, semantic search, or AI agents need to fall back to @aws-sdk/client-s3vectors from npm, losing the performance benefits and ergonomic API that Bun's native S3 client provides.
Use cases that would benefit:

Building RAG applications with cost-effective vector storage (up to 90% cheaper than specialized vector DBs)
AI agent memory at scale (up to 2 billion vectors per index)
Semantic search across documents, images, or media
Integration with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases

What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
S3 Vectors is now generally available in 14 AWS regions (https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/amazon-s3-vectors-generally-available/).
Add native S3 Vectors support to Bun's S3 client, similar to how the existing Bun.S3Client provides a fast, ergonomic API for standard S3 operations.
A potential API could look like:
import { S3VectorsClient } from "bun";

const vectors = new S3VectorsClient({
  accessKeyId: "...",
  secretAccessKey: "...",
  region: "us-west-2",
});

// Create a vector index
await vectors.createIndex({
  vectorBucketName: "my-vectors",
  indexName: "embeddings",
  dimension: 1024,
  distanceMetric: "cosine",
});

// Insert vectors
await vectors.putVectors({
  vectorBucketName: "my-vectors",
  indexName: "embeddings",
  vectors: [
    { key: "doc-1", data: { float32: embedding }, metadata: { genre: "scifi" } }
  ]
});

// Query similar vectors
const results = await vectors.query({
  vectorBucketName: "my-vectors",
  indexName: "embeddings",
  queryVector: { float32: queryEmbedding },
  topK: 10,
  filter: { genre: "scifi" },
});
What alternatives have you considered?
Using @aws-sdk/client-s3vectors from npm works but loses the performance advantages and clean API that Bun's native implementation provides for standard S3 operations.

