What is the problem this feature would solve?
When testing discriminated unions, the expect().toBe() call doesn't narrow the TypeScript type, so you need an extra if block or a separate assertion:
const result = Either.try(() => validate(input))

expect(result._tag).toBe("Left")
// TypeScript still thinks result could be Right
if (result._tag === "Left") {
  expect(result.left.reason).toBe("InvalidInput")
}
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Have expect(value).toBe(expected) use TypeScript's asserts signature so it narrows the type:
interface Expect<T> {
  toBe<V extends T>(expected: V): asserts this is Expect<V>
}
This would allow:
expect(result._tag).toBe("Left")
// TypeScript now knows result._tag is "Left", narrowing result to Left
expect(result.left.reason).toBe("InvalidInput")
What alternatives have you considered?
Using Node's assert.strictEqual which does have the asserts signature, but mixing assertion styles is awkward.

