Compare the execution time for the test files below.
Because expect(promise).resolves blocks the event loop, rather than using proper asynchronous code, it blocks other tests from executing. This causes concurrent test suites that heavily depend on expect.resolves to take enormous amounts of time. On my machine, the test suite took 20x longer to execute compared to when I manually awaited the operation beforehand.
While I understand that busy waiting is the intended behaviour in bun (#15457, #14950), I really do question this design decision. Asynchronous code should not be made synchronous, or all sorts of issues like this will crop up.
// expect.resolves.test.ts
// Ran 100 tests across 1 file. [100.15s]
import { expect, test } from 'bun:test';

async function slowOperation() {
  await new Promise(r => (void setTimeout(r, 1000)));
  return { ok: true };
}

test.concurrent.each(
  [...Array(100).keys()],
)('expect.resolve', () => {
  expect(slowOperation())
    .resolves.toStrictEqual({ ok: true });
});
// await-expect.test.ts
