What is the problem this feature would solve?
Intervals expressed as raw multiplications like const interval = 6 * 60 * 60 * 1000 are difficult to understand at a glance.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Named constants for common time intervals in Bun's standard library. For example:
/**
 * Time units represented in milliseconds.
 *
 * @example
 * // 3 minutes in milliseconds
 * const interval = 3 * Time.MINUTES_MS;
 */
class Time {
    /** Number of milliseconds in one second. */
    static SECONDS_MS = 1000;

    /** Number of milliseconds in one minute. */
    static MINUTES_MS = 60 * Time.SECONDS_MS;

    /** Number of milliseconds in one hour. */
    static HOURS_MS = 60 * Time.MINUTES_MS;

    /** Number of milliseconds in one day. */
    static DAYS_MS = 24 * Time.HOURS_MS;
}
It could also include variants ending with _SEC for scenarios where intervals are expected in seconds.
This approach is inspired by similar patterns found in Golang's time package.
What alternatives have you considered?
I use that Time class in every project. It's perfectly fine to just define it every time, but it's a general enough pattern to have it available in the standard library.
I'm open to contributing for this feature.

What is the problem this feature would solve?
While using Bun's WebSocket primites, I often have to write small utility functions like:
function sendJson(socket: Bun.ServerWebSocket, data: any) {
    socket.send(JSON.stringify(data));
}
Essentially the WebSocket equivalent of Response.json().
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
While functions that take the socket as an argument work fine, I would like to be able to extend the default ServerWebSocket object and add custom methods to it.
While Bun's WebSocket primitives are fantastic, being able to easily extend it would enable developers to better adapt the API to their domain-specific needs. Custom methods could improve code organization and simplify overall architecture. It would also be more egonomic as the function doesn't need to be imported in other files.
What alternatives have you considered?
I was able to hack this together by adding the method to the data field, but it's far from ideal.
If I need access to the socket object itself, I cannot define the method at server.upgrade. I got around this by defining an empty implementation and re-assigning it to the actual function in the open function:
interface SocketCtx {
  sendJson(data: any): void;
}

Bun.serve({
  routes: {
    '/ws': (request, server) => {
      server.upgrade(request, {
        data: {
          sendJson() {
            throw new Error('Unimplemented');
          },
        },
      });
    },
  },
  websocket: {
    data: {} as SocketCtx,
    open(socket) {
      socket.data.sendJson = (data: any) => {
        socket.send(JSON.stringify(data));
      };
    },
    close() {},
    message() {},
  },
});
While this approach works, it's pretty hacky as these methods should be immutable.
I'm open to contributing for this feature.

What is the problem this feature would solve?
Currently, Bun lacks built-in primitives for synchronization and mutual exclusion. This becomes critical when:

Coordinating access to shared resources (files, sockets, databases)
Preventing race conditions in concurrent operations
Implementing critical sections in multi-worker environments
Building rate limiters or connection pools

Without native locking, developers must rely on external packages or implement their own solutions using Redis/filesystem locks, which adds unnecessary complexity and dependencies.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Add native locking primitives to Bun's standard library:
import { lock } from "bun";

// Simple mutex
await lock.acquire("resource-id");
try {
  // critical section
} finally {
  await lock.release("resource-id");
}

// With timeout
await lock.acquire("resource-id", { timeout: 5000 });

// Read-Write locks for better concurrency
await lock.rlock(async () => {
  // multiple readers allowed simultaneously
  await readResource();
});

await lock.wlock(async () => {
  // exclusive writer access
  await writeResource();
});

// Scoped/auto-release syntax
await lock.with("resource-id", async () => {
  // auto-releases on return/throw
});
This would be implemented using Bun's native runtime for optimal performance, possibly leveraging OS-level primitives or futexes, maybe even with an optional redis integration.
What alternatives have you considered?

External packages like async-mutex or semaphore - adds dependencies, not optimized for Bun
Redis-based locks - requires external service, adds network latency
File-based locks - slow I/O operations, cleanup issues on crashes
Worker-based solutions - complex setup, overhead for simple use cases

Native support would provide better performance, simpler code, and align with Bun's "batteries-included" philosophy.

