What is the problem this feature would solve?
Currently, there is no way to call async functions from within a SQLite transaction when using Database.transaction(). There may be some need to perform, in a transaction, a SELECT, then some async operation (e.g., an API request), then an INSERT/UPDATE/etc. However, bun:sqlite affords no way to do this at present.
Here is an example:
import { Database } from "bun:sqlite";

async function sleep(ms: number) {
	return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}

const db = new Database();

db.run(`CREATE TABLE foo (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY)`);

setTimeout(() => {
	// This runs second
	db.run(`INSERT INTO foo VALUES (2)`);
	// This runs third
	console.log("a", db.query(`SELECT count(*) FROM foo`).get());
}, 1000);

db.transaction(async () => {
	// This runs first
	db.run(`INSERT INTO foo VALUES (1)`);
	await sleep(3000);
	// This runs fourth
	console.log("b", db.query(`SELECT count(*) FROM foo`).get());
})();
count(*) is 2 in both SELECT queries, but it should be 1 in both since the transaction should be isolated until it is committed. In reality, since Database.transaction() has no awareness of async functions, the callback will execute up until the first await and will then return a Promise, causing the transaction to be committed immediately after the insert, whereas it should not commit until after the select.
What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?
Database.transaction() should support async functions (i.e., functions returning Promise), and properly await the returned Promise, or simply do the post-transaction commit as a Promise.then() callback.
